Observed failure: Tab past the last visible control (#upload) goes to #help link outside modal because focus trap fails due to hidden retry button? Let's examine observed steps:

Sequence:
- After opening modal, focus moves to #filename (first focusable)
- Then tab -> #upload (button)
- Then tab -> expected #modal-close but actually got #help (link in footer)

Thus focus trap didn't wrap to first focusable element within modal; instead went out to next tabbable element in DOM after modal (the help link).

Why? In micromodal's maintainFocus method, they check if shift+Tab and forward tab wrap using focusableNodes list derived from getFocusableNodes(). However note that the modal includes a hidden button #retry with class "retry" and display:none via CSS .retry{display:none}. This button is still in the DOM and is focusable? It's a button not disabled, not hidden via aria-hidden, but CSS display:none makes it not visible and also not in tab order? Actually display:none removes it from accessibility tree and it is not focusable (cannot be tabbed to). However the getFocusableNodes selector includes 'button:not([disabled]):not([aria-hidden])' which would still match the button even if display:none because it doesn't check visibility. So the hidden button is included in focusableNodes list, making it part of the tab order logic. Since it's not actually focusable (due to display:none), when tabbing from #upload, the focusableNodes list order might be: #filename, #upload, #retry, #modal-close? Wait order in DOM: inside modal container: header (h2, button), then p label/input, then button #upload, then button #retry (hidden), then modal closes? Actually after upload button there is retry button then closing? Let's look at HTML:

<div class="modal__container" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-labelledby="upload-title">
  <div class="modal__header">
    <h2 id="upload-title">Upload report</h2>
    <button id="modal-close" type="button" aria-label="Close upload dialog" data-micromodal-close>&times;</button>
  </div>
  <p><label for="filename">File name</label><br><input id="filename" type="text" value="report.pdf"></p>
  <button id="upload" type="button">Upload</button>
  <button id="retry" class="retry" type="button">Try again</button>
</div>

So order: modal-close button (close), filename input, upload button, retry button.

When modal opens, focus is set to first node which is not a close trigger. The close trigger is the button with data-micromodal-close (modal-close). So nodesWhichAreNotCloseTargets excludes modal-close button. The focusableNodes list includes all focusable elements: a[href] (none inside modal), area, input, select, textarea, button:not([disabled]):not([aria-hidden]), iframe, object, embed, [contenteditable], [tabindex]:not([tabindex^="-"]). So inside modal we have: modal-close button (has data-micromodal-close), filename input, upload button, retry button. Also maybe the overlay has tabindex="-1" but not focusable due to negative.

getFocusableNodes returns all those nodes in document order within modal: modal-close, filename, upload, retry.

Now nodesWhichAreNotCloseTargets filters out nodes with attribute data-micromodal-close => removes modal-close button. So list becomes: filename, upload, retry.

If nodesWhichAreNotCloseTargets length >0, focus first element: filename. Good.

Now maintainFocus: on keydown Tab (not shift). It checks if !event.shiftKey && focusableNodes.length >0 && focusedItemIndex === focusableNodes.length -1 then focus first and preventDefault.

But note: they use focusableNodes (the full list including close trigger) not nodesWhichAreNotCloseTargets. That's important.

In our case, focusableNodes = [modal-close, filename, upload, retry].

When focus is on #upload (index 2). focusableNodes.length =4. focusedItemIndex=2. Not equal to length-1 (3). So condition fails, no wrap, focus moves to next element in DOM which is #retry button. However #retry is not focusable due to display:none, so browser skips it and goes to next focusable element in DOM after modal: the #help link. That explains the failure.

Thus the bug: maintainFocus should consider only focusable nodes that are actually focusable (i.e., not disabled, not hidden via CSS?). But easier: exclude nodes that are not focusable due to being hidden (like display:none) or maybe they should exclude nodes that have the close trigger attribute AND also nodes that are not focusable due to being hidden. However simplest fix: In maintainFocus, when checking for wrap, we should use the same filtered list as used for setting initial focus (nodesWhichAreNotCloseTargets) OR we should filter out nodes that are not focusable due to being hidden (like having CSS display none). But we cannot compute computed style easily. However we can rely on the fact that the hidden button has class "retry" which is styled display:none via CSS. We could add a condition to ignore nodes that have attribute indicating they are hidden? Not reliable.

Better approach: modify getFocusableNodes to exclude elements that are not focusable due to being hidden via CSS? That's complex.

Simpler: In maintainFocus, when determining if we are at last focusable node, we should consider only nodes that are not disabled and not hidden via aria-hidden and also not matched by close trigger? Actually the original logic likely intended to treat close trigger as not part of the tab circle (they want focus to skip close button when wrapping). They already filter out close triggers for initial focus but not for wrap detection. So we should also filter out close triggers in maintainFocus wrap condition.

Thus change maintainFocus to use focusableNodesWithoutClose = focusableNodes.filter(node => !node.hasAttribute(this.config.closeTrigger)). Then use that list for wrap detection and focusing.

